Alice and Bob have got a binary∗ string s of length n. They have decided to play a game on it, taking turns alternately, with Alice moving first.

In each move, the player must select a subsequence† which has an odd number of inversions‡ and delete it. The player who cannot make a move loses.
Determine who wins the game, assuming both players play optimally.
∗A binary string is a string that consists only of the characters 0 and 1.
†A sequence a is a subsequence of a string b if a can be obtained from b by the deletion of several (possibly zero or all) characters.
‡An inversion in a binary string s is a pair of indices (i,j) such that i<j and si=1 and sj=0.

Input:
The first line contains a single integer t (1≤t≤104) — the number of test cases. Description of each test case follows.
The first line of each test case contains a single integer n (1≤n≤2⋅105) — the length of the binary string s.
The second line of each test case contains a binary string s of length n. It is guaranteed that each character of s is either 0 or 1.
It is guaranteed that the sum of n over all the test cases does not exceed 2⋅105.

Output:
For each test case, print Alice if Alice wins the game and Bob otherwise.

Strategy:
The main idea is that since the number of inversions of the removed subsequence needs to be odd, we can do case analysis on what's going be left to ensure there are no possible good moves when the array is returned to bob on move 2. The trivial win is if there are an odd number of inversion to begin with. Alice can immediately remove the whole array and win. If there is an odd number of inversions, we have 2 cases, either there are no odd weight elements (meaning they add an odd number of inversion to the total) or there is >=1 of odd weight elements. If there is >=1 odd weight elements the idea is simple. Since the total number of inversions is even and there is at least 1 element that has an odd number of inversions. This implies we can remove everything except that element (any of the odd ones). Since even-odd=odd and total-(odd weight)=odd_leftover we can delete everything except that one odd weight element and end up with an odd subsequence that we are deleting. Since 1 element has 0 inversions bob loses. The only way Alice loses is if there are no odd weight elements at all. This is because she each player either loses on the spot because they can't do anything or they need to remove a subsequence with an odd number of inversions. Each removed subsequence incurs 2 costs on the total. The number of inversion inside the subsequence and the number of inversions between {inside, outside} pairs. Since the number of inversions inside the subsequence must be odd, for the case where the total is even we have even=odd+cross+remaining. Since we know each element causes an even number of inversions we can divided the number into the amount it causes with other deleted elements and the amount it causes with elements that stay. Since the total is even these have the same parity. Now if we sum this total up for all deleted elements we get the total total sum be even (since even times any integer is even)=sum(out)+sum(in) now these sum still have to share parity. Since sum(in) counts an inversion for all zeroes after a 1 and then again for all ones after a zero it must be even. Therefore the the number of cross inversions is also even. Now we plug in even=odd+even+remaining which forces remaining to be odd which means bob will just delete the whole thing and win.